it's not easy to get to pakistan's zara  booty district  few people know their way around the  rough terrain  and for years most of the country was  unaware of the community's struggle  hundreds of people including children  have lost their limbs  because of explosive devices hidden in  the dry land  from a years-long conflict  now for the first time in years many are  walking again  on their own a local charity is giving  artificial limbs to more than a hundred  people  galib hussein is one of them he was 13  when he lost his legs 12 years ago  i took my goats and sheep to the  mountains that day but i didn't know  there were explosive devices in the area  suddenly there was a huge blast and i  lost my legs my parents are very poor  so they couldn't afford any treatment  i've had to stay home for the last 12  years  the bombs were left behind from a  conflict between pakistani forces and  the ballot liberation army  the fight goes back decades the  separatist group wants autonomy for the  region  explosives were placed in the ground  through the years but is mostly poor and  marginalized people  who've suffered the consequences many  have died  others left disabled the worst part was  this is too far off and the people are  too poor they're like hundreds more  patients who came up today  they wanted to see legs as you saw today  those people who are running around this  you know they must have  have influence on other people and they  came from the mountains as well they  want the artificial limbs  shazia is trying out her new prosthetic  leg  her uncle is nearby in case she falls  she says she wore her favorite dress to  mark the day when she walked again  she was four years old when an explosive  tore off her leg  she had to stop going to school but now  she says she wants to go back  and one day be a doctor  i used to see other children walk to  school every day but i couldn't go with  them  i would see them playing but i couldn't  join i'm very fond of learning but i had  to stop my studies  but now i can go back to school i can  walk again  the path ahead may still be challenging  but people here say they now look  forward to walking through it  on their own katya lopezodayan  al jazeera
 

